lean": 0.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.6}, "rating_margin": 8.17, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.83, "baseline_margin": 7.72, "analyst_adjustment": -0.72, "key_factors": ["Vasquez's large cash advantage ($2.4M vs $0.8M) and incumbent perks", "Markets price Vasquez near 90-94% despite a narrow polling edge", "Ratings consensus Likely D with a Lean D minority; district is even PVI in a Trump-won seat", "National attention and attack ads keep the race competitive"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a clear but not overwhelming Vasquez edge: incumbency, a big fundraising lead, and a Likely D rating consensus, while markets price him around 90-94%. The lone recent poll shows only a three-point race, so the margin stays modest rather than double-digit.", "watch": "A late wave of outside spending against Vasquez, or another poll confirming the race in the low single digits, would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Vasquez defends a southern New Mexico seat that is even on partisanship but was carried by Trump, making it one of the most contested Democratic-held districts. He has heavily out-raised Cunningham and holds a large cash advantage, and national Democrats have already aired ads to protect him. Cunningham has drawn support from Speaker Johnson and national Republicans, and attack ads have flooded the district. Ratings mostly call the race Likely D, with a few at Lean or Tilt D. Markets put Vasquez's chances around 90-94%, while the only recent poll shows a narrow three-point lead. The forecast lands at a modest Democratic margin with elevated uncertainty, consistent with a Likely D race that remains within reach for Republicans if the environment breaks their way.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8708, "margin": 7.0}, {"dat